  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday) \nIssued at 145 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe showers and thunderstorms mentioned in the short term\nforecast above are expected to linger across much of West Central\nTexas through Monday afternoon as the upper-level low begins to \ntrack further eastward and out of the forecast area by early\nTuesday morning. Highest rainfall totals for Sunday night into \nMonday are expected to remain south of a San Angelo to San Saba \nline, with our focus for heavy rainfall and potential for flooding\nremaining across the I-10 corridor. WPC currently has the area \nsouth of a San Angelo to San Saba line outlooked under a Marginal \nRisk for Excessive rainfall, with portions of Kimble, Mason, and\nSutton Counties falling into the Slight Risk area.\n\nGlobal models are showing another disturbance traversing the base\nof the large scale upper-level trough mid to late next week, and\ntracking through portions of West Central Texas. However, models\ncontinue to show most of the precipitation falling to our east \nand remaining on the drier side of this system under north to\nnorthwesterly flow. Temperatures are expected to remain near\nseasonal norms with lows in the 50s dropping into the mid 40s to\nlow 50s by mid to late next week, and highs in the upper 60s to \nmid 70s.",
